\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Hover at (a) a horizontal stroke plane and (b) an inclined stroke plane (drawn here with $\gamma < 0$). The wing is drawn over one full wingbeat at equal time increments, with the $-\hat  {s}$ half-stroke behind, in darker grey, and the $+\hat  {s}$ half-stroke in front. In (a), the reference pitch kinematics ($\psi _0 = 0^\circ $) set the mean force $\langle \vec  {F}^* \rangle $ vertically along $\hat  {n}$. In (b), the mean force remains vertical while $\hat  {n}$ is tilted because $\psi _0^\text  {hover}$ is trimmed to bias $\langle \vec  {F}^* \rangle $ by $\beta = -\gamma ^\text  {hover}$ using the inverse of \autoref {eq:beta-hover}.}}{14}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hover-beta-gamma}{{10}{14}{Hover at (a) a horizontal stroke plane and (b) an inclined stroke plane (drawn here with $\gamma < 0$). The wing is drawn over one full wingbeat at equal time increments, with the $-\hat {s}$ half-stroke behind, in darker grey, and the $+\hat {s}$ half-stroke in front. In (a), the reference pitch kinematics ($\psi _0 = 0^\circ $) set the mean force $\langle \vec {F}^* \rangle $ vertically along $\hat {n}$. In (b), the mean force remains vertical while $\hat {n}$ is tilted because $\psi _0^\text {hover}$ is trimmed to bias $\langle \vec {F}^* \rangle $ by $\beta = -\gamma ^\text {hover}$ using the inverse of \autoref {eq:beta-hover}}{figure.caption.11}{}}
